# Scoped to this directory ON PURPOSE. gitattributes borrows gitignore's scoping: a .gitattributes
# applies to its own directory and everything below it, so these rules cover the odin-sims project and
# nothing else in bridge-bidding-system. The repository root has no .gitattributes, and putting
# `* text=auto eol=lf` there would reach every .html, .csv and image in the rest of the repo - a
# repository-wide renormalization to settle a problem that is only the Odin toolchain's. Adding this
# file here costs the rest of the repo nothing.
#
# LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working directory, on every platform. This matches the Odin
# project's own .editorconfig, which declares `end_of_line = lf` despite Odin being developed mainly on
# Windows.
#
# `text=auto` alone only guarantees LF in the *index*; what lands in the working directory is then left
# to each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which Git for Windows sets to `true` in its system config. That
# gives Windows checkouts CRLF files while .editorconfig (end_of_line = lf) and the tools that rewrite
# files in place - odinfmt above all - emit LF, so a formatting pass leaves a file with mixed endings
# and Git reports "<line-endings changed since checkout>". `eol=lf` pins the working directory too and
# overrides core.autocrlf, so the whole toolchain agrees. Odin, just, cmd and Sublime all read LF
# happily on Windows.
#
# Every tracked file under this directory was already LF in both the index and the working tree when
# this was added, so it pins the status quo rather than rewriting anything - no `git add --renormalize`
# needed. That is only true because odinfmt.json now sets `"newline_style": "LF"`; odinfmt does NOT
# read .editorconfig's `end_of_line`, and without that setting it writes CRLF on Windows and re-dirties
# the tree after every `just format`.
#
# Keep this in sync with .editorconfig's `end_of_line` and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the exception: some interpreters need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch the contents of these. They are gitignored today, so this only matters for a file
# committed deliberately or force-added - but that is exactly the case where the `* text=auto eol=lf`
# above would otherwise corrupt one. The HTML/PNG entries are here because this project generates deal
# pages and hand-diagram images beside its sources.
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.lib binary
*.dll binary
*.obj binary
*.o binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.bc binary
*.spall binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
